# 7. Employee login and account management

The changes to `backend/app/auth.py` and `backend/app/routers/users.py` address the earlier findings.

The current code includes checks for deleted or deactivated login records and preserves the account's active status during password resets.

These are the correct directions.

However, I would still require the following tests before considering employee access management complete.

| Test                                                 | Expected result                                           |
| ---------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |
| Employee is deactivated while logged in              | Subsequent protected requests are rejected                |
| Login record is deleted                              | Existing session is rejected                              |
| Employee password is reset                           | Account remains inactive if it was inactive               |
| Employee profile is created but login creation fails | Profile is marked inactive and the failure is recoverable |
| Two employee creation requests arrive together       | Duplicate accounts are prevented                          |
| Salesman requests another employee's salary          | Access is denied                                          |
| Salesman attempts to approve production              | Access is denied                                          |
| PA attempts to create an Owner                       | Access is denied                                          |
| Employee role is changed                             | Old session cannot continue using stale privileges        |

The most important principle is that **backend authorization must use current account permissions, not just the role that was present when the employee logged in**.

